{"name":"Wild Mustard Seed","hex":"#caa867","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wild","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Untamed; not domesticated.","Especially, being of the wild type: being of an unbroken ancestral line of undomesticated animals, as opposed to being feral, being an undomesticated animal whose ancestors were domesticated."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Inaccurately; not on target.","Intended to be synchronized with film or video but recorded separately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild"},{"term":"mustard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of certain species of the genus Brassica, or of related genera (especially Sinapis alba, in the family Brassicaceae, with yellow flowers, and linear seed pods).","Powder or paste made from seeds of the mustard plant, and used as a condiment or a spice."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark yellow colour.","Very good, or very skilled."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mustard"},{"term":"seed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any propagative portion of a plant which may be sown, such as true seeds, seed-like fruits, tubers, or bulbs.","A fertilized and ripened ovule, containing an embryonic plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To plant or sow an area with seeds.","To shed seeds (refers to plants)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seed"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mustard (condiment)","blocks":[{"text":"Mustard is a condiment made from the seeds of a mustard plant, which may be the white/yellow mustard, brown mustard, or black mustard."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Mustard.JPG/330px-Mustard.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mustard_(condiment)","wikidata":"Q131748"},{"title":"Mustard seed","blocks":[{"text":"Mustard seeds are the small round seeds of various mustard plants. The seeds are usually about 1 to 2 millimeters in diameter and may be colored from yellowish white to black. They are an important spice in many regional foods and may come from one of three different plants: black mustard, brown mustard (B. juncea), or white mustard."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Mustard.png/330px-Mustard.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mustard_seed","wikidata":"Q1937700"},{"title":"Brassicaceae","blocks":[{"text":"Brassicaceae or Cruciferae is a medium-sized and economically important family of flowering plants commonly known as the mustards, the crucifers, or the cabbage family. Most are herbaceous plants, while some are shrubs. The leaves are simple, lack stipules, and appear alternately on stems or in rosettes. The inflorescences are terminal and lack bracts. The flowers have four free sepals, four free alternating petals, two shorter free stamens and four longer free stamens. The fruit has seeds in rows, divided by a thin wall. As of 2023, the family was divided into two subfamilies, Brassicoideae and Aethionemoideae, and as of September 2025, it contained over 350 accepted genera."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Barbarea_vulgaris_002.JPG/330px-Barbarea_vulgaris_002.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brassicaceae","wikidata":"Q156888"},{"title":"Seed","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a seed is a plant structure containing an embryo and stored nutrients in a protective coat called a testa. More generally, the term seed means anything that can be sown, which may include seed and husk or tuber. Seeds are the product of the ripened ovule, after the embryo sac is fertilized by sperm from pollen, forming a zygote. The embryo within a seed develops from the zygote and grows within the mother plant to a certain size before growth is halted."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BE%D0%B1%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BC%D1%8F%D0%BD.jpg/330px-%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BE%D0%B1%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BC%D1%8F%D0%BD.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seed","wikidata":"Q40763"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Senf-Variationen_edit2.jpg/500px-Senf-Variationen_edit2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Senf-Variationen_edit2.jpg/1280px-Senf-Variationen_edit2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Senf-Variationen_edit2.jpg/500px-Senf-Variationen_edit2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mustard seeds (top left) may be ground (top right) to make different kinds of mustard. These four mustards are: English mustard with turmeric colouring (centre left), a Bavarian sweet mustard (centre right), a Dijon mustard (lower left), and a coarse French mustard made mainly from black mustard seeds (lower right).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASenf-Variationen_edit2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Catla_fish_in_mustard_curry_-_Kolkata_-_West_Bengal.jpg/500px-Catla_fish_in_mustard_curry_-_Kolkata_-_West_Bengal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Catla_fish_in_mustard_curry_-_Kolkata_-_West_Bengal.jpg/1280px-Catla_fish_in_mustard_curry_-_Kolkata_-_West_Bengal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia